# /etc/svc/sshd.hamsh — Hamnix-native SSH-2.0 server, managed service.
#
# Read by the init-side service supervisor (user/hamsh.ad's
# builtin_svc). `svc start sshd` in /etc/rc.boot parses this file,
# registers the entry in the service table, and forks /bin/sshd in
# PID 1's default namespace. The supervisor reaps the child if it
# exits and restarts it per the policy below.
#
# Format (data, not code): key:value lines, '#' to EOL is a comment.
# Required: name, ns, exec. Required ns values: init | <captured-ns>.

name: sshd
ns: init
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `uid:` LINE HERE, AND THIS IS NOT A RELAXATION
# OF docs/security.md PHASE 11. IT IS HOW PHASE 11 FINALLY HOLDS.
#
# This file used to say `uid: 2`, and the intent was right: a compromise
# of sshd must not be a compromise of the host owner. What it produced was
# a service that never ran. The supervisor rforks and calls sys_setuid
# BEFORE spawn() (user/hamsh.ad, builtin_svc), so uid 2 was in force
# before sshd's first instruction -- and port 22 is privileged, so
# net_announce() failed, restart-on-failure respawned it on a backoff, and
# the console filled with the same four lines for ever:
#
#     [sshd] Hamnix SSH-2.0 server starting
#     [sshd] WARN: could not persist host key (volatile this boot)
#     [sshd] host key ready (ecdsa-sha2-nistp256)
#     [sshd] net_announce() failed
#
# The same uid is why it could not write its host key, so even had it
# listened it would have offered a new host identity every boot.
#
# A uid the supervisor sets is necessarily set BEFORE the program starts.
# That is the right shape for a service that never needs privilege and the
# wrong one for a service that needs it exactly once, at startup, and then
# never again. The knowledge of WHEN it is safe to drop belongs to the
# program, so sshd does the standard thing: it creates /var/lib/ssh,
# persists its host key, binds port 22 -- all while privileged -- and then
# calls sys_setuid(2) and only afterwards accepts its first connection. It
# logs `dropped privilege to uid 2 before serving`, and treats a failure
# to drop as FATAL rather than a warning, because a daemon that says it
# dropped and did not is worse than one that never claimed to.
#
# So every byte of peer-controlled input is still handled at uid 2, which
# is what Phase 11 asked for, and the listen succeeds, which under `uid: 2`
# it could not. See the SSHD_UID block at the top of user/sshd.ad.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated because it is a real cost and not a footnote: a
# session at uid 2 has no CAP_SYS_ADMIN, so it cannot create a private
# namespace. `enter linux { ... }` over SSH reports
# `rfork: no private namespace yet (needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN)`. Ordinary
# commands, pipelines and the shell itself are unaffected -- measured
# against a running guest. That is the price Phase 11 chose to pay, and
# raising the uid to buy it back is a product decision, not a bug fix.
exec: /bin/sshd
restart: on-failure
backoff_ms: 1000
backoff_max_ms: 30000
description: Hamnix SSH-2.0 server (curve25519-sha256 + chacha20-poly1305)
